  faq={[
    { q: 'What does the CSS light-dark() function do?', a: 'It takes two colour values and returns the first when the used colour scheme is light and the second when it is dark. It lets a single declaration carry both themes, so a stylesheet no longer needs a duplicate block inside a prefers-color-scheme media query.' },
    { q: 'Why is light-dark() not working?', a: 'Almost always because color-scheme has not been declared. The function reads the used colour scheme of the element, and without color-scheme: light dark on :root that value stays light, so light-dark() silently returns its first argument and everything looks like the light theme.' },
    { q: 'Does light-dark() replace prefers-color-scheme?', a: 'For colours, largely yes. For anything that is not a colour it does not, because the function only returns colour values. A different image, a different border radius or a hidden element per scheme still needs the media query.' },
